<blockquote>
<p><em>RIP=20
<a href=3D"https://markforster.squarespace.com/" title=3D"Mark Forster's Ge=
t Everything Done">Mark Forster</a> (1943-2025).
We first met Mark (on the internet) in 2012, which is also when=20
<a href=3D"https://www.beeminder.com/mforster1uk" title=3D"Mark Forster's B=
eeminder gallery">he started using</a> Beeminder.
Forster=E2=80=99s systems and insights have
<a href=3D"https://blog.beeminder.com/tags/mark%20forster" title=3D"Beemind=
er blog posts tagged 'Mark Forster'">featured in many Beeminder blog posts<=
/a> over the years.
But the best of all hasn=E2=80=99t appeared until now.
Years ago we collaborated with Mark on a guest post by him, to be published=
 here on the Beeminder blog, with a definitive version of the system he cal=
led =E2=80=9CFinal Version=E2=80=9D.
Final Version didn=E2=80=99t turn out to quite be the final version of his =
system, which is why we sat on this draft.
But it=E2=80=99s the version we=E2=80=99ve always liked best and is what we=
 mean when we refer to <em>Forster Lists</em> (or sometimes <em>Dot Lists</=
em>).
So without further ado, here=E2=80=99s Mark Forster in his own words (and l=
ight editing from us) to explain the system.</em></p>
</blockquote>
<h2>Quick Start</h2>
<ol>
<li>Add tasks to the bottom of your to-do list as you think of them</li>
<li>When ready to work on your tasks, put a dot in front of the very first =
task on the list</li>
<li>Continue down the list until you hit a task you want to do (not must do=
) before doing the task you just dotted</li>
<li>Put a dot in front of that task and repeat until you reach the end of t=
he list</li>
<li>You now have a chain of tasks with dots in front of them</li>
<li>Do these dotted tasks from bottom to top</li>
<li>You can either complete a task and cross it off or <em>start</em> a tas=
k, cross it off, and re-add it at the bottom</li>
<li>If urgent tasks come up, add them to the bottom with a dot, so they=E2=
=80=99re done first</li>
</ol>
<h2>Quicker Start</h2>
<p><em>Hello from the future.
Forster Lists probably work best on paper but for any hands-on learners, I,=
 Bethany, implemented a little web version of the system that might even be=
 self-explanatory:</em></p>
<center>
<a href=3D"https://bsoule.com/dotlist/">
<span style=3D"background-color:#1d76db;color:#FFF;padding:0 16px;font-size=
:16px;font-weight:600;line-height:3;border-radius:4px;font-family:-apple-sy=
stem,BlinkMacSystemFont,'Segoe UI',Helvetica,Arial,sans-serif;display:inlin=
e-block !important">
Bee=E2=80=99s Forster List App
</span>
</a>
</center>

<p><em>Back to Mark Forster=E2=80=A6 (and more from us at the end)</em></p>
<h2>Introduction</h2>
<p>Here are the long-awaited instructions for the Final Version (FV) time m=
anagement system.=20
I don=E2=80=99t know if it=E2=80=99s the best time management system ever d=
evised.=20
What I do know is that it is the best time management system that I [writin=
g circa 2012-2015] have ever used myself.=20
It=E2=80=99s shown itself to be resilient, responsive, and very quick. </p>
<p>FV is based on my earlier time management systems, particularly the exte=
nsive range of AutoFocus and SuperFocus systems developed over the last thr=
ee years.=20
These were unique in that they were constantly developing with the assistan=
ce of a large band of commenters on my website.=20
Anyone who has tried one or more of these systems will recognize the strong=
 family resemblance that they have with FV.=20
The most striking resemblance is that they are all based on one long list (=
either paper or electronic) which can be used to capture just about every p=
ossible action that springs into one=E2=80=99s mind.=20
There is a minimum of special markings or annotations.</p>
<p>Such a list depends on an effective algorithm to process it.=20
There are three main requirements which have to be kept in balance.=20
These are=20
<strong>urgency</strong>,=20
<strong>importance</strong>, and=20
<strong>psychological readiness</strong>.=20
Traditional time management systems have tended to concentrate on the first=
 two of these.=20
The neglect of psychological readiness is probably the reason that most peo=
ple don=E2=80=99t find time management systems particularly effective or co=
ngenial.</p>
<p>The most distinctive feature of FV is the way that its algorithm is <em>=
primarily</em> based on psychological readiness=E2=80=89=E2=80=94=E2=80=89t=
his then opens the way to keeping urgency and importance in the best achiev=
able balance. </p>
<h2>The FV Algorithm</h2>
<p>The FV algorithm is loosely based on two powerful decision making method=
s=E2=80=89=E2=80=94=E2=80=89
<a href=3D"https://www.structuredprocrastination.com/" title=3D"Original es=
say by John Perry">structured procrastination</a> and=20
<a href=3D"https://markforster.squarespace.com/blog/2007/2/5/the-resistance=
-principle-and-colleys-rule.html" title=3D"Heuristic for decision-making wh=
en choices come in sequence: reject the first option but remember it as a b=
enchmark, then accept the first choice you encounter that beats the benchma=
rk">Colley=E2=80=99s rule</a>=E2=80=89=E2=80=94=E2=80=89though neither need=
 be understood to use the finished algorithm.</p>
<p>The FV algorithm uses the question=20
<em>=E2=80=9CWhat do I want to do before I do x?=E2=80=9D</em>=20
to preselect a chain of tasks from the list.=20
What exactly is meant by =E2=80=9Cwant=E2=80=9D in this context is delibera=
tely left undefined.=20
There may be a whole variety of reasons why you might want to do one thing =
before another thing and all of them are valid.</p>
<p>The chain always starts with the <strong>first unactioned task</strong> =
on the list.=20
Mark this task with a dot to show that it=E2=80=99s now been preselected.=
=20
Don=E2=80=99t take any action on the task at this stage.</p>
<p>This task then becomes the benchmark from which the next task is selecte=
d.=20
For example, if the first task on the list is =E2=80=9CWrite Report=E2=80=
=9D, the question becomes=20
<em>=E2=80=9CWhat do I want to do before I write the report?=E2=80=9D</em>=
=20
You move through the list in order until you come to a task which you want =
to do (not =E2=80=9Cmust do=E2=80=9D) before writing the report.=20
This task is now selected by marking it with a dot and it becomes the bench=
mark for the next task.=20
If the first task you come to which you want to do before writing the repor=
t is =E2=80=9CCheck Email=E2=80=9D, then that becomes the benchmark.=20
The question therefore changes to=20
<em>=E2=80=9CWhat do I want to do before I check email?=E2=80=9D</em></p>
<p>As you continue through the list you might come to =E2=80=9CTidy Desk=E2=
=80=9D and decide you want to do that before checking email.=20
Select this in the same way by marking it with a dot, and change the questi=
on to=20
<em>=E2=80=9CWhat do I want to do before tidying my desk?=E2=80=9D</em>=20
The answer to this is probably =E2=80=9Cnothing=E2=80=9D, so you have now f=
inished your preselection.</p>
<p>The preselected tasks in the example are:</p>
<pre><code>   Write report
   Check email
   Tidy desk
</code></pre>
<p>Do these in <em>reverse order</em>, i.e. Tidy desk, Check email, Write r=
eport.=20
Note that as in all my systems, you don=E2=80=99t have to finish the task=
=E2=80=89=E2=80=94=E2=80=89only do some work on it.=20
Of course if you do finish the task that=E2=80=99s great, but if you don=E2=
=80=99t then all you have to do is re-enter the task at the end of the list=
.</p>
<p>Once you have taken action on all the preselected tasks, preselect anoth=
er chain of tasks starting again from the first unactioned task on the list=
.</p>
<p>That=E2=80=99s it! You=E2=80=99re now ready to go.=20
Everything else is further examples and explanation.</p>
<h2>A Longer Example</h2>
<p>In this example for ease of understanding no new tasks are added while w=
orking on the list.=20
This of course is unlikely in real life. </p>
<p>Your initial list of tasks:</p>
<pre><code>   Email=20
   In-Tray
   Voicemail
   Project X Report
   Tidy Desk
   Call Dissatisfied Customer
   Make Dental Appointment
   File Invoices
   Discuss Project Y with Bob
   Back Up =20
</code></pre>
<p>Put a dot in front of the first task:</p>
<pre><code> =E2=80=A2 Email
   In-Tray
   Voicemail
   Project X Report
   Tidy Desk
   Call Dissatisfied Customer
   Make Dental Appointment
   File Invoices
   Discuss Project Y with Bob
   Back Up
</code></pre>
<p>Now ask yourself=20
<em>=E2=80=9CWhat do I want to do before I do Email?=E2=80=9D</em></p>
<p>You work down the list and come to Voicemail.=20
You decide you want to do Voicemail before doing Email.=20
Put a dot in front of it as well:</p>
<pre><code> =E2=80=A2 Email=20
   In-Tray
 =E2=80=A2 Voicemail
   Project X Report
   Tidy Desk
   Call Dissatisfied Customer
   Make Dental Appointment
   File Invoices
   Discuss Project Y with Bob
   Back Up =20
</code></pre>
<p>Now ask yourself=20
<em>=E2=80=9CWhat do I want to do before I do Voicemail?=E2=80=9D</em>=20
You decide you want to tidy your desk.  </p>
<pre><code> =E2=80=A2 Email=20
   In-Tray
 =E2=80=A2 Voicemail
   Project X Report
 =E2=80=A2 Tidy Desk
   Call Dissatisfied Customer
   Make Dental Appointment
   File Invoices
   Discuss Project Y with Bob
   Back Up =20
</code></pre>
<p>There are no tasks you want to do before tidying your desk, so now take =
action on the dotted tasks in reverse order:  </p>
<pre><code>   Tidy Desk
   Voicemail
   Email =20
</code></pre>
<p>Your list will now look like this (I=E2=80=99ve removed the tasks that h=
ave been actioned but if you are using paper they will still be on the page=
 but crossed out):  </p>
<pre><code>   In-Tray
   Project X Report
   Call Dissatisfied Customer
   Make Dental Appointment
   File Invoices
   Discuss Project Y with Bob
   Back Up =20
</code></pre>
<p>Now start again with the first unactioned task on the list, In-Tray, and=
 repeat the same procedure.=20
The only task you want to do before In-Tray is Back Up.=20
As this is the last task on the list there are only two dotted tasks: </p>
<pre><code> =E2=80=A2 In-Tray
   Project X Report
   Call Dissatisfied Customer
   Make Dental Appointment
   File Invoices
   Discuss Project Y with Bob
 =E2=80=A2 Back Up
</code></pre>
<p>Do the two dotted tasks in reverse order:</p>
<pre><code>   Back Up
   In-Tray
</code></pre>
<p>So the list now looks like this:</p>
<pre><code>   Project X Report
   Call Dissatisfied Customer
   Make Dental Appointment
   File Invoices
   Discuss Project Y with Bob
</code></pre>
<p>So far the tasks have been relatively trivial, but the Project X Report =
is something that you have been putting off doing for a long time.=20
So repeat the procedure:</p>
<pre><code> =E2=80=A2 Project X Report
   Call Dissatisfied Customer
 =E2=80=A2 Make Dental Appointment
 =E2=80=A2 File Invoices
   Discuss Project Y with Bob
</code></pre>
<p>You now file your invoices, make a dental appointment and make a start o=
n the Project X Report.</p>
<p>In your final pass through the list you Discuss Project Y with Bob and C=
all Dissatisfied Customer.</p>
<p>So the tasks on the original list have been done in the following order.=
=20
The tasks in italics are the ones at the beginning of each scanning process=
.</p>
<pre>
   Tidy Desk
   Voicemail
   <i>Email</i>
  =20
   Back Up
   <i>In-Tray</i>
   =20
   File Invoices
   Make Dental Appointment
   <i>Project X Report</i>
  =20
   Discuss Project Y with Bob
   <i>Call Dissatisfied Customer</i>
</pre>

<p>Notice what has happened here.=20
The root tasks (the ones in italics) have been done in strict list order, r=
egardless of importance, urgency or any other factor.=20
Some of them are relatively easy (e.g. Email) and some are relatively diffi=
cult (e.g. Project X Report) or you are reluctant to do them (e.g. Call Dis=
satisfied Customer).</p>
<p>Each of the root tasks is preceded by a short ladder of tasks which are =
in the order in which you want to do them.=20
The number and difficulty of the tasks in the ladder tend to reflect the di=
fficulty of the root tasks.</p>
<h2>Additional Tips</h2>
<p>The best way to sink any time management system is to overload it right =
at the beginning.=20
FV is pretty resilient, but at this stage you aren=E2=80=99t.=20
So build up the list gradually.=20
My advice is to start off with the tasks and projects that are of immediate=
 concern to you right now, and then add more as they come up in the natural=
 course of things.</p>
<p>Tasks can be added at any level, e.g. Project X, Plan Restructuring, Cal=
l Pete, Tidy Desk. </p>
<p>If the first task on the list <em>can=E2=80=99t</em> be done now for som=
e valid reason=20
(e.g. wrong time of day, precondition not met, bad weather),=20
then cross it out and re-enter it at the end of the list.
Use the next task as your starting benchmark.</p>
<p>If at any stage you find that a task on the list is no longer relevant, =
then delete it. </p>
<p>If you find that your preselected list is no longer relevant (e.g. if yo=
u have had a long break away from the list),=20
then scrap the preselection and reselect from the beginning.=20
A shorter way to do this is to reselect only from the last preselected task=
 which you haven=E2=80=99t done yet.</p>
<p>If one or more very urgent things come up, write them at the end of the =
list and mark them with a dot so that they are done next.=20
If something already on the list becomes very urgent, then move it to the e=
nd of the list and mark it with a dot in the same way.</p>
<p>Remember that the aim of any time management system is to help you to ge=
t your work done, not get in the way of doing your work.=20
So don=E2=80=99t be afraid to adjust priorities if you need to.=20
However try to keep this to a minimum=E2=80=89=E2=80=94=E2=80=89stick to th=
e rules whenever possible as they will ensure you deal with your work in a =
systematic way.</p>
<h2>Why It Works</h2>
<p>At the beginning of this article I said there were three factors which e=
very time management system needs to address:=20
urgency,=20
importance and=20
psychological readiness.=20
Let=E2=80=99s see how FV deals with each of these.</p>
<p><strong>Urgency.</strong>
Because of the nature of the preselection process, urgent tasks tend to get=
 selected=E2=80=89=E2=80=94=E2=80=89generally speaking the human brain want=
s to do things that it knows are urgent.=20
If things come up that are particularly urgent they can be added to the pre=
selected list at any time.</p>
<p><strong>Importance.</strong>
Generally speaking the human brain is a bit less keen on doing important st=
uff than it is on doing urgent stuff.=20
This is particularly the case when the important stuff is difficult.=20
However the FV preselection process ensures that tasks towards the beginnin=
g of the list are given as much attention as tasks towards the end of the l=
ist.</p>
<p><strong>Psychological Readiness.</strong>
This is where FV really enters new dimensions.=20
By using a preselection process, the brain is softened up towards the selec=
ted tasks.=20
But this isn=E2=80=99t all.=20
The selection process is based on what you <em>want</em> to do.=20
This colours the whole preselected list so that even the first task, which =
you may not have wanted to do at all, gets affected.=20
In addition, doing the list in reverse order, with the least wanted task la=
st, uses structured procrastination to get the tasks done.</p>
<p><br>&nbsp;<br></p>
